SMART INCUBATOR MOTHERBOARD WIRING AND TEST GUIDE
=================================================

This guide describes the current firmware pin map and the safe order for
connecting and testing every part. Read the complete guide before connecting
the heater or any high-current load.


1. IMPORTANT SAFETY RULES
-------------------------

1. Disconnect USB and every external power supply before changing wires.
2. ESP32 GPIO pins are signal pins only. Never power a fan, heater, motor,
   humidifier, relay coil, or servo directly from a GPIO pin.
3. Use 3.3 V for DHT22, DS18B20, and compatible RTC sensor power.
4. Use separate, correctly rated supplies for the heater, fans, servo,
   humidifier, and egg-turning motor.
5. Non-isolated drivers must share signal ground with the ESP32.
6. Put a correctly rated fuse close to each load power supply.
7. Put an independent thermal fuse or mechanical thermostat in series with
   the PTC heater. Firmware is not a replacement for hardware protection.
8. Do not use the low-voltage wiring in this guide for mains AC equipment.
9. Use thick wire and rated terminals for heater and motor current. Do not use
   a breadboard for high-current paths.
10. Run the incubator empty for at least 48 hours before adding eggs.


2. COMPLETE ESP32 PIN MAP
-------------------------

INPUTS AND SENSORS

GPIO 4  : DHT22 at the small air intake
GPIO 5  : DHT22 in the middle of the eggs
GPIO 16 : DS18B20 in the middle of the eggs, main temperature control
GPIO 17 : DS18B20 attached to the PTC heater, overheat protection
GPIO 19 : Normally-closed lid limit switch
GPIO 21 : I2C SDA for DS1307 RTC
GPIO 22 : I2C SCL for DS1307 RTC
GPIO 0  : ESP32 BOOT button and 8-second factory-reset button

OUTPUTS

GPIO 13 : Exhaust vent servo signal
GPIO 14 : Egg tray roller relay/driver signal
GPIO 25 : Circulation fan 1 driver signal
GPIO 26 : Circulation fan 2 driver signal
GPIO 27 : Exhaust fan PWM driver signal, 1 kHz
GPIO 32 : PTC heater PWM driver signal, 1 kHz
GPIO 33 : Humidifier relay/driver signal

IDLE OUTPUT LEVELS

GPIO 14, 25, 26, 27, 32, and 33 are OFF after boot. Digital outputs use:

OFF = approximately 0 V
ON  = approximately 3.3 V

The exhaust and heater PWM outputs may show an average voltage between 0 V
and 3.3 V when their sliders are between 0% and 100%.


2A. REQUIRED COMPONENTS
-----------------------

SENSORS AND INPUTS

2 x DHT22 temperature/humidity sensors
2 x DS18B20 temperature probes
2 x 4.7 kOhm DS18B20 DATA pull-up resistors
2 x 4.7-10 kOhm DHT22 DATA pull-up resistors for bare sensors
1 x DS1307 RTC module
1 x normally-closed mechanical lid limit switch
4 x 100 nF ceramic sensor bypass capacitors

OUTPUT CONTROL

2 x fan MOSFET/driver modules for circulation fans
1 x PWM MOSFET/driver module for the exhaust fan
1 x high-current PWM MOSFET/driver module for the PTC heater
1 x relay/MOSFET/SSR driver suitable for the humidifier
1 x relay/MOSFET/motor driver suitable for the egg-turning motor
1 x regulated 5 V servo for the exhaust vent

If a bare logic-level N-channel MOSFET is used instead of a driver module:

1 x 100-220 ohm resistor in series with each MOSFET gate
1 x 10 kOhm resistor from each MOSFET gate to source/GND
1 x correctly rated flyback diode for each inductive motor or relay coil
1 x correctly sized heatsink for each high-current MOSFET

POWER AND PROTECTION

Regulated ESP32 USB/5 V supply
Regulated 5 V servo supply
Correct external DC supply for the heater, fans, humidifier, and motor
One correctly rated fuse for the heater circuit
One correctly rated fuse for each other high-current supply branch
One independent thermal fuse or thermostat in series with the PTC heater
One 470-1000 uF electrolytic capacitor near the servo connector
One 100 nF ceramic capacitor near the servo connector
Screw terminals and wire rated for the actual load current

Load current calculation:

Current in amps = Load power in watts / Supply voltage

Example: 100 W / 12 V = 8.3 A. Select the fuse, wire, terminal, MOSFET,
heatsink, and supply for the real continuous and startup current. Do not choose
a fuse only from this example.


2B. COMPLETE LOW-VOLTAGE BLOCK DIAGRAM
--------------------------------------

This diagram is for low-voltage DC loads only.

                              +----------------------+
                              |        ESP32         |
                              |                      |
 Air-intake DHT22 DATA -------| GPIO 4               |
 Egg-zone DHT22 DATA ---------| GPIO 5               |
 Egg DS18B20 DATA ------------| GPIO 16              |
 PTC DS18B20 DATA ------------| GPIO 17              |
 Lid NC switch ---------------| GPIO 19              |
 RTC SDA ---------------------| GPIO 21              |
 RTC SCL ---------------------| GPIO 22              |
                              |                      |
 Vent servo signal -----------| GPIO 13              |
 Egg-turner driver IN --------| GPIO 14              |
 Fan 1 driver IN -------------| GPIO 25              |
 Fan 2 driver IN -------------| GPIO 26              |
 Exhaust PWM driver IN -------| GPIO 27              |
 Heater PWM driver IN --------| GPIO 32              |
 Humidifier driver IN --------| GPIO 33              |
                              |                      |
 Sensor VCC ------------------| 3.3 V                |
 All signal grounds ----------| GND                  |
                              +----------------------+

SENSOR POWER BUS

 ESP32 3.3 V ----+---- DHT22 intake VCC
                 +---- DHT22 egg-zone VCC
                 +---- DS18B20 egg VCC
                 +---- DS18B20 PTC VCC
                 +---- RTC VCC when the module supports 3.3 V

 ESP32 GND ------+---- all sensor GND wires
                 +---- lid switch NC return
                 +---- all non-isolated driver signal grounds

LOAD POWER BUS

 External supply + ---- fuse ----+---- heater/fan/motor/load positive
 External supply - --------------+---- driver power ground
                                  +---- ESP32 GND for non-isolated drivers

Never connect external load voltage to an ESP32 GPIO pin.


2C. RESISTOR AND CAPACITOR MAP
------------------------------

DHT22 intake pull-up:

 ESP32 3.3 V ---- 4.7-10 kOhm ----+---- GPIO 4
                                  |
                                  +---- DHT22 intake DATA

DHT22 egg-zone pull-up:

 ESP32 3.3 V ---- 4.7-10 kOhm ----+---- GPIO 5
                                  |
                                  +---- DHT22 egg DATA

Egg DS18B20 pull-up:

 ESP32 3.3 V ---- 4.7 kOhm -------+---- GPIO 16
                                  |
                                  +---- Egg DS18B20 DATA

PTC DS18B20 pull-up:

 ESP32 3.3 V ---- 4.7 kOhm -------+---- GPIO 17
                                  |
                                  +---- PTC DS18B20 DATA

Sensor bypass capacitor, one per sensor:

 Sensor VCC --------+
                    | |
                 100 nF
                    | |
 Sensor GND --------+

Bare MOSFET gate resistors:

 ESP32 GPIO ---- 100-220 ohm ---- MOSFET Gate
                                      |
                                   10 kOhm
                                      |
 MOSFET Source / GND -----------------+

PC817 optocoupler input for GPIO 33:

 GPIO 33 ---- 330-470 ohm ---- PC817 input LED anode
 ESP32 GND ------------------- PC817 input LED cathode

Servo supply filtering:

 Servo 5 V ----+---- Servo VCC
               |
               +---- 470-1000 uF ---- Servo GND
               |
               +---- 100 nF --------- Servo GND

Do not add another pull-up when a sensor module already includes the correct
pull-up and the resulting parallel resistance would be too low.


3. SENSOR WIRING
----------------

AIR-INTAKE DHT22, GPIO 4

VCC  -> ESP32 3.3 V
DATA -> ESP32 GPIO 4
GND  -> ESP32 GND

 ESP32 3.3 V ---------------- DHT22 VCC
 ESP32 GPIO 4 --------------- DHT22 DATA
 ESP32 GND ------------------ DHT22 GND
       3.3 V -- 4.7-10 kOhm -- GPIO 4

This sensor measures outside/incoming air temperature and humidity. It is an
environmental reference and humidity fallback.

EGG-ZONE DHT22, GPIO 5

VCC  -> ESP32 3.3 V
DATA -> ESP32 GPIO 5
GND  -> ESP32 GND

 ESP32 3.3 V ---------------- DHT22 VCC
 ESP32 GPIO 5 --------------- DHT22 DATA
 ESP32 GND ------------------ DHT22 GND
       3.3 V -- 4.7-10 kOhm -- GPIO 5

This sensor measures air temperature and humidity around the eggs. Its
humidity is the main chamber humidity measurement. Its temperature is a slow
cross-check for the egg-zone DS18B20.

For each bare four-pin DHT22, looking at the front grille with pins down:

Pin 1 -> 3.3 V
Pin 2 -> DATA
Pin 3 -> Not connected
Pin 4 -> GND

Add one 4.7 kOhm to 10 kOhm resistor between DATA and 3.3 V for each bare
DHT22. A three-pin DHT22 module may already contain this resistor; follow the
VCC, DATA/S, and GND labels printed on the module.

EGG-ZONE DS18B20, GPIO 16

VCC/Red    -> ESP32 3.3 V
DATA       -> ESP32 GPIO 16
GND/Black  -> ESP32 GND
4.7 kOhm resistor between GPIO 16 and 3.3 V

 ESP32 3.3 V ---------------- DS18B20 VCC
 ESP32 GPIO 16 -------------- DS18B20 DATA
 ESP32 GND ------------------ DS18B20 GND
       3.3 V -- 4.7 kOhm ---- GPIO 16

Place this probe at egg-center height without touching an egg, wall, heater,
or humidifier mist. This is the main temperature used by heater PWM control.

PTC-HEATER DS18B20, GPIO 17

VCC/Red    -> ESP32 3.3 V
DATA       -> ESP32 GPIO 17
GND/Black  -> ESP32 GND
4.7 kOhm resistor between GPIO 17 and 3.3 V

 ESP32 3.3 V ---------------- DS18B20 VCC
 ESP32 GPIO 17 -------------- DS18B20 DATA
 ESP32 GND ------------------ DS18B20 GND
       3.3 V -- 4.7 kOhm ---- GPIO 17

Attach this probe firmly to the PTC heater with a heat-rated clamp or suitable
heat-resistant material. The firmware reduces heater PWM from 50 C to 60 C
and forces the heater OFF at 60 C.

Waterproof DS18B20 cable colors are not guaranteed. Verify the seller's
pinout before applying power.


4. RTC AND LID SWITCH
---------------------

DS1307 RTC

RTC VCC -> ESP32 3.3 V
RTC GND -> ESP32 GND
RTC SDA -> ESP32 GPIO 21
RTC SCL -> ESP32 GPIO 22

 ESP32 3.3 V ---------------- RTC VCC
 ESP32 GND ------------------ RTC GND
 ESP32 GPIO 21 -------------- RTC SDA
 ESP32 GPIO 22 -------------- RTC SCL

The expected I2C address is 0x68. Do not allow RTC pull-up resistors to pull
SDA or SCL to 5 V.

NORMALLY-CLOSED LID SWITCH

GPIO 19 -> switch COM
GND     -> switch NC

 ESP32 GPIO 19 -------- COM [ limit switch ] NC -------- ESP32 GND

 Lid closed  : COM touches NC, GPIO 19 reads LOW
 Lid open    : COM leaves NC, GPIO 19 reads HIGH
 Broken wire : GPIO 19 reads HIGH and the firmware treats the lid as open

Lid closed means COM and NC are connected. Lid open, a disconnected wire, or
a failed switch is treated as an open lid. Heater, humidifier, and egg turner
are then disabled. Heater tests require the lid switch to report CLOSED.


5. OUTPUT DRIVER WIRING
-----------------------

The signal side and load side are different. The ESP32 connects only to the
driver input. The fan, heater, motor, or humidifier connects to the driver's
load output.

GENERAL DRIVER SIGNAL CONNECTION

 ESP32 GPIO -------- Driver IN / PWM / TRIG
 ESP32 GND --------- Driver signal GND

Never connect a driver LOAD/OUT terminal to an ESP32 GPIO.

CIRCULATION FAN 1

GPIO 25 -> Fan 1 MOSFET/driver signal input
ESP GND -> Driver signal GND
Fan     -> External supply through the driver load terminals

Typical low-side fan driver:

 External supply + -------------------------- Fan +
 Fan - ------------------------------- Driver LOAD-/OUT-
 External supply - -------------------- Driver power GND
 ESP32 GND ---------------------------- Driver signal GND
 ESP32 GPIO 25 ------------------------ Driver IN

Typical bare logic-level N-channel MOSFET:

 GPIO 25 ---- 100-220 ohm ---- Gate
                              |
                            10 kOhm
                              |
 GND ----------------------- Source
 Fan - --------------------- Drain
 Fan + ---- fuse ----------- Supply +
 Supply - ------------------ GND

For an inductive brushed motor, place a correctly rated flyback diode across
the motor: diode stripe/cathode to motor positive, anode to motor negative.

CIRCULATION FAN 2

GPIO 26 -> Fan 2 MOSFET/driver signal input
ESP GND -> Driver signal GND
Fan     -> External supply through the driver load terminals

Use the same circuit as Fan 1, but connect the control input to GPIO 26.

EXHAUST FAN

GPIO 27 -> Exhaust MOSFET driver PWM/TRIG input
ESP GND -> Driver signal GND
Fan     -> External supply through the driver load terminals

The exhaust output is 1 kHz PWM. The exhaust vent servo opens whenever exhaust
PWM is above 0% and closes when exhaust PWM returns to 0%.

Exhaust fan diagram:

 GPIO 27 -------------------- Driver PWM/TRIG
 ESP32 GND ------------------ Driver signal GND
 Supply + ---- fuse --------- Exhaust fan +
 Exhaust fan - -------------- Driver switched output
 Supply - ------------------- Driver power GND

PTC HEATER

GPIO 32       -> Heater MOSFET driver PWM/TRIG input
ESP GND       -> Driver signal GND
Supply +      -> Fuse -> PTC heater +
PTC heater -  -> Driver switched load output
Supply -      -> Driver power GND

Use a MOSFET driver rated above the heater's continuous current. GPIO 32 is a
1 kHz PWM signal, not a heater power output.

PTC heater diagram:

 GPIO 32 -------------------- Heater driver PWM/TRIG
 ESP32 GND ------------------ Heater driver signal GND

 Supply + ---- heater fuse ---- thermal cutoff ---- PTC heater +
 PTC heater - ------------------------------------- Driver LOAD-/OUT-
 Supply - ----------------------------------------- Driver power GND

The independent thermal cutoff must physically sense the heater and must work
when the ESP32 is disconnected or crashed.

HUMIDIFIER

GPIO 33 -> Humidifier relay/MOSFET/SSR control input
ESP GND -> Driver signal GND when the driver is not isolated

An optocoupler alone cannot power a humidifier. The optocoupler must drive a
correctly rated relay, MOSFET, or SSR. Use a 330-470 ohm resistor in series
with a basic PC817 input LED when driven from 3.3 V.

Low-voltage humidifier with a non-isolated MOSFET module:

 GPIO 33 -------------------- Driver IN
 ESP32 GND ------------------ Driver signal GND
 Supply + ---- fuse --------- Humidifier +
 Humidifier - --------------- Driver switched output
 Supply - ------------------- Driver power GND

PC817 plus a separate load driver:

 GPIO 33 -- 330-470 ohm -- PC817 LED anode
 ESP GND ----------------- PC817 LED cathode

 Isolated supply + -- 10 kOhm --+---- Load driver input
                                |
 PC817 collector ---------------+
 PC817 emitter ----------------- Isolated supply GND

The PC817 output normally inverts the signal in this example. Use a driver
circuit that gives the required fail-safe OFF state. Do not connect a
humidifier directly to the PC817 output.

EGG TRAY ROLLER

GPIO 14 -> Motor relay/driver signal input
ESP GND -> Driver signal GND
Motor   -> External motor supply through the driver

Use flyback protection for a DC motor or mechanical relay coil.

Egg-turner motor diagram:

 GPIO 14 -------------------- Motor driver/relay IN
 ESP32 GND ------------------ Driver signal GND
 Supply + ---- fuse --------- Motor/relay load +
 Motor/relay load - --------- Driver switched output
 Supply - ------------------- Driver power GND

For a bare relay coil, connect a flyback diode directly across the coil:

 Relay coil + ---------------- Diode cathode/stripe
 Relay coil - ---------------- Diode anode

EXHAUST VENT SERVO

GPIO 13 -> Servo signal
Servo VCC -> External regulated 5 V
Servo GND -> External 5 V supply GND
ESP GND   -> External 5 V supply GND

Do not power the servo from ESP32 3.3 V. The servo closes at approximately 10
degrees and opens at approximately 90 degrees.

Servo diagram:

 ESP32 GPIO 13 ---------------- Servo signal
 External regulated 5 V ------- Servo VCC
 External supply GND ---------- Servo GND
 ESP32 GND -------------------- External supply GND

                             +---- 470-1000 uF ----+
 Servo 5 V ------------------+                     +---- Servo GND
                             +---- 100 nF ---------+


6. COMMON GROUND
----------------

For non-isolated low-voltage drivers, connect these grounds together:

ESP32 GND
Fan supply negative
Heater supply negative
Servo supply negative
Egg-turner driver ground
Humidifier driver signal ground

Do not connect grounds across a genuinely isolated optocoupler barrier unless
the selected driver circuit specifically requires it.


7. FIRST POWER-ON AND SENSOR TEST
---------------------------------

1. Disconnect every output driver and load.
2. Connect only the ESP32 by USB.
3. Upload the normal incubator firmware.
4. Confirm GPIO 14, 25, 26, 27, 32, and 33 are approximately 0 V.
5. Power off.
6. Connect the two DHT22 sensors and two DS18B20 sensors.
7. Power on and open the serial monitor.
8. Confirm neither DS18B20 shows -127 C or 85 C.
9. Confirm both DHT22 sensors show real temperature and humidity.
10. Connect the RTC and lid switch only after all temperature sensors work.

Serial monitor command:

pio device monitor --port COM5 --baud 115200

Close the serial monitor with Ctrl+C before uploading firmware.


8. HARDWARE CONNECTIONS APP TEST
--------------------------------

The Hardware connections page uses a boot-specific test session. Old Firebase
commands are ignored after an ESP restart. Every output starts OFF after boot.

1. Start the ESP32 and wait until the app says ESP32 ONLINE.
2. Open Hardware connections.
3. Verify all switches and sliders are OFF/0%.
4. Enable hardware test mode.
5. Test only one output at a time.
6. Press ALL OFF before moving to the next output.
7. Leaving the page also sends ALL OFF.

FAN 1 TEST

1. Leave loads disconnected for the first voltage test.
2. Turn Circulation fan 1 ON.
3. Measure GPIO 25 to ESP32 GND: approximately 3.3 V.
4. Turn it OFF: approximately 0 V.
5. Connect the driver and fan only after this passes.

FAN 2 TEST

Repeat the same steps on GPIO 26.

EXHAUST AND SERVO TEST

1. Move the exhaust PWM slider above 0%.
2. GPIO 27 should output PWM.
3. The exhaust fan should run.
4. The vent servo should move toward 90 degrees.
5. Return the slider to 0%; fan stops and servo closes near 10 degrees.

HEATER TEST

The heater slider is intentionally blocked unless:

Lid switch is CLOSED
Egg DS18B20 on GPIO 16 is valid
PTC DS18B20 on GPIO 17 is valid
At least one DHT22 humidity value is valid
No hardware fault is latched
No incubation cycle is active

1. Test GPIO 32 with a multimeter before connecting the heater.
2. Start at 5-10% PWM.
3. Confirm PTC temperature rises slowly.
4. Confirm PWM is reduced as PTC temperature approaches 50-60 C.
5. Never perform the first heater test unattended.

HUMIDIFIER TEST

1. Keep the heater OFF.
2. Turn the humidifier ON briefly.
3. Confirm GPIO 33 changes from 0 V to approximately 3.3 V.
4. Confirm egg-zone humidity begins increasing after the normal sensor delay.
5. Keep both DHT22 sensors away from direct mist.

EGG TURNER TEST

1. Remove eggs.
2. Turn Egg tray roller ON briefly.
3. Confirm GPIO 14 changes to approximately 3.3 V.
4. Check direction, mechanical stops, and current before a long test.

MAINTAIN ATMOSPHERE TEST

Use this only with an empty incubator.

1. Enter a target temperature and target humidity.
2. Press Start maintain test.
3. The ESP automatically controls heater PWM, humidifier, circulation fans,
   exhaust fan, and vent servo.
4. Watch egg temperature, egg humidity, and PTC heater temperature.
5. Press Stop maintain test or ALL OFF when finished.


9. AUTOMATIC INCUBATION PRIORITY
--------------------------------

Manual Hardware connections controls are for testing only. Automatic cycle or
calibration logic has priority. Starting either mode disables manual testing.

During automatic operation:

Egg DS18B20 controls heater PWM.
Egg-zone DHT22 controls chamber humidity.
Intake DHT22 reports incoming air and provides degraded humidity fallback.
PTC DS18B20 limits heater temperature.
Circulation fans mix chamber air.
Exhaust fan and vent remove excess heat/humidity.
Egg turning stops at the configured lockdown day.
The app requests replacement of the roller tray with the hatching tray.


10. BUILD, UPLOAD, AND APP COMMANDS
-----------------------------------

Build firmware:

pio run -e nodemcu-32s

Upload firmware:

pio run -e nodemcu-32s --target upload --upload-port COM5

If upload stays at Connecting, hold BOOT, start upload, and release BOOT when
Writing at appears.

Run Flutter app:

Set-Location -LiteralPath "C:\SmartIncubator\app"
flutter run -d emulator-5554

Build an icon-safe debug APK:

flutter build apk --debug --no-tree-shake-icons


11. TROUBLESHOOTING
-------------------

OUTPUT BLINKS OR DOES NOT FOLLOW APP

Confirm test telemetry reports firmware build:

2026-08-11-output-test-v6

Restart the app after firmware upload. The app must receive the new
testSessionId before a switch can send commands. Old commands are ignored.

FAN RUNS WHEN GPIO IS 0 V

The driver is active-low or the load is wired around the switched output.
Check the driver documentation and load terminals.

DS18B20 SHOWS -127 C

The probe is disconnected, wired incorrectly, or missing its 4.7 kOhm pull-up.

DS18B20 SHOWS 85 C

The conversion was not completed or the probe has a wiring/power problem.

DHT22 SHOWS NULL/NAN

Check 3.3 V, GND, DATA pin, pin orientation, and pull-up resistor. Allow at
least two seconds between readings.

ESP RESTARTS OR BECOMES HOT

Disconnect all external hardware immediately. Test with USB only. Reversed
power, a short, or external voltage entering a GPIO can damage the ESP32.

RTC FREEZES STARTUP

Check SDA/SCL polarity and ensure neither line is pulled to 5 V or GND. The RTC
must appear at I2C address 0x68.


12. REQUIRED EMPTY-INCUBATOR VALIDATION
---------------------------------------

Before adding eggs:

1. Run empty for 48 continuous hours.
2. Compare readings with a trusted calibrated thermometer/hygrometer.
3. Check temperatures at the center and all tray corners.
4. Verify lid opening forces heater/humidifier/turner OFF.
5. Disconnect each sensor one at a time and confirm safe shutdown.
6. Verify PTC overtemperature derating and the independent thermal cutoff.
7. Test power failure and restart behavior.
8. Test the full setting-to-hatching tray transition without eggs.

Do not trust the incubator with eggs until every test in this guide passes.
